Row 3 on its own: whether this buffer is a vault note, and whether the
atlas database is reachable. Nothing else in the bar is about where you
are in the system, so it gets its own line rather than competing with the
buffer name for row 1.

** Reading the database
`my/manifolding-atlas-db-health' is the right source and is unusually
well-behaved: it returns `(STATE . HINT)' and documents that it never
signals, because infrastructure state is meant to be reported rather than
raised. It does open a connection, though — so on a phone with no
PostgreSQL running it will report DOWN every time, and that is the normal
case here, not a fault. The accent reflects that: DOWN is the expected
offline state and is drawn in the calm blue, while the states that mean
"this is misconfigured" get the red.

** Segment
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defvar manifolding-modeline--atlas-cache nil
  "Cached atlas row text, or nil when there is nothing to say.")

(defun manifolding-modeline--atlas-state ()
  "Short word for the current database state, or nil if the Atlas is absent."
  (when (fboundp 'my/manifolding-atlas-db-health)
    (condition-case nil
        (let ((state (car (my/manifolding-atlas-db-health))))
          (pcase state
            ('CONNECTED "db ok")
            ('DOWN "db off")
            ('AUTH-FAILED "db auth")
            ('DATABASE-MISSING "db none")
            ('SCHEMA-OUTDATED "db schema")
            (_ nil)))
      (error nil))))

(defun manifolding-modeline--atlas-notes-p ()
  "Non-nil when the current buffer is a file inside the vault."
  (and (fboundp 'my/manifolding-atlas-git--in-notes-p)
       (buffer-file-name)
       (condition-case nil
           (and (my/manifolding-atlas-git--in-notes-p buffer-file-name) t)
         (error nil))))

(defun manifolding-modeline--atlas-refresh ()
  "Recompute the atlas row."
  (setq manifolding-modeline--atlas-cache
        (let ((db (manifolding-modeline--atlas-state))
              (note (manifolding-modeline--atlas-notes-p)))
          (cond ((and db note) (concat "vault " db))
                (db db)
                (note "vault")
                (t nil)))))

(defun manifolding-modeline--atlas-alert-p ()
  "Non-nil when the database needs intervention rather than a start command."
  (and (fboundp 'my/manifolding-atlas-db-health)
       (memq (condition-case nil
                 (car (my/manifolding-atlas-db-health))
               (error nil))
             '(AUTH-FAILED DATABASE-MISSING SCHEMA-OUTDATED))))

(manifolding-modeline-define-segment atlas
  :doc "Vault membership and database state."
  ;; The face is chosen here rather than declared, because it depends on
  :face nil
  :side :left
  :construct
  (lambda ()
    (let ((text manifolding-modeline--atlas-cache))
      (and text
           (list :propertize text
                 (list 'face (if (manifolding-modeline--atlas-alert-p)
                                 'manifolding-modeline-alert
                               'manifolding-modeline-info))))))
  :refresh '(manifolding-modeline--atlas-refresh)
  :slow t)
#+end_src

** Cost
`db-health' opens a connection, which makes this by far the most expensive
segment in the bar — on a phone with no PostgreSQL running it will report
DOWN, and the attempt itself is the cost. So it registers on the *slow*
refresh hook and is probed once every `manifolding-modeline-slow-every'
ticks (five minutes at the defaults), never on the 30-second tick and never
on a redisplay. The vault-membership half of the segment is just a path
comparison and would be happy to run every tick; the two are kept together
because splitting them would mean two segments to place in the order file
for one line of text.

If it still proves too chatty on the device, drop `atlas` from row 3 in the
`order' file — no code change, which is what putting layout there is for.
